  "title": "Container With Most Water",
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "categories": ["two-pointers", "greedy", "array"],
  "descriptionMd": "You are given an array `height` where `height[i]` is the height of a vertical wall standing at position `i` on the x-axis.\n\nChoose **two** walls. Together with the x-axis they form a container whose capacity is the distance between the walls multiplied by the height of the **shorter** wall (water would spill over the shorter one). Return the largest capacity you can achieve. The container cannot be tilted.\n\n**Example 1**\n\n```\nheight = [1, 8, 6, 2, 5, 4, 8, 3, 7]\n→ 49\n```\nWalls at positions 1 and 8 are 7 apart and the shorter one has height 7, so `7 · 7 = 49`.\n\n**Example 2**\n\n```\nheight = [1, 1]\n→ 1\n```",
  "constraints": ["2 ≤ height.length ≤ 10⁵", "0 ≤ height[i] ≤ 10⁴"],
  "hints": ["Start with the widest possible container: the first and last walls.", "When you move one wall inward the width shrinks. Moving which wall could possibly lead to a bigger capacity?"],
  "signature": {
    "functionName": "maxArea",
    "params": [{ "name": "height", "type": "int[]" }],
    "returns": "int"
  },
